<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Termessos</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 70 BCE (approx.) · capital Termessos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Termessos, a fortified Pisidian city high in the Taurus mountains above Antalya, which Alexander the Great chose not to attack.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Termessos was built at over 1,000 metres on a mountain north-west of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a>, traditionally by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solymi">Solymi</a>, an Anatolian people said to have fought the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a>. Its site guarded a pass between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a> and the Pisidian highlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was governed by its own council and assembly and was famous for its fierce independence. In 333 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> tried to force the pass but gave up the siege. Termessos later fought its neighbours and became a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 71 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> granted Termessos the status of free ally by law, keeping its self-government. It remained an autonomous city within the Roman world and was abandoned after an earthquake in late antiquity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/termessos.jpg" alt="Termessos" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Ingo Mehling · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Termessos_-_Theater.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 70 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Termessos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek and Pisidian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Solymian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Termessus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-235&amp;focus=termessos" aria-label="Termessos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5444 845 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Termessos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5764 1101L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5764 1101h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5764 1101h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-235&amp;focus=termessos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

